<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-water-and-lipids" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Water and Lipids</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      All the food chemistry we will discuss might seem incredibly daunting.
      It is important to understand that it really just boils down to manipulating four different families of molecules.
      Even better, we have already encountered all of them.
      Here, we reintroduce all four classes of molecules, going deeper into what atoms and bonds exist within them, and discussing how they behave in a hot pan.
    </p>

    <p>
      We will now go back where we started <mdash/> water and lipids <mdash/> but now in more detail, particularly exploring their applications in the kitchen.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-water-acts-as-a-universal-medium">
    <title>Water Acts as a Universal Medium</title>

    <p>
      Food is mostly water.
      Meat is about 75% water, and vegetables about 90% water.
      We have already discussed the molecular properties of water in <xref ref="sec-water-and-oil"/>.
      As we know, it is able to attract polar molecules, thus dissolving polar nutrients.
      Water is also able to move heat through food via conduction and convection.
    </p>

    <p>
      Recall from <xref ref="sec-heat-and-temperature"/> that the boiling point of water is <quantity><mag>100</mag><unit base="celsius"/></quantity>.
      This means that as long as water is present on the surface of food, the food cannot exceed that temperature.
      We will need to address this problem for reactions like deep crisping and browning.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-lipids-help-with-high-heat-cooking">
    <title>Lipids Help With High-Heat Cooking</title>

    <p>
      We have previously mentioned lipids before <mdash/> like the discussion of oils in <xref ref="sec-water-and-oil"/>, the protective lipids in the skin in <xref ref="sec-body-wash"/>, or the different lipids present in moisturizers in <xref ref="sec-moisturizers-and-lotions"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Lipids can be surprisingly hard to define, but for our purposes, the word "lipid" contains substances like oils, fats, steroids, waxes, and other small hydrophobic or amphiphilic molecules.
      <idx><h>lipid</h></idx>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-triglycerides">
      <caption>The structure of a triglyceride molecule.</caption>
      <image source="images/ch-03-the-kitchen/sec-water-and-lipids/triglycerides.png" width="80%"/>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The most abundant lipid in body fat and vegetable fat is the <term>triglyceride</term>.
      It is built from glycerol and three hydrocarbon chains called fatty acids.
      Recall that glycerol is used as a humectant ( <xref ref="subsec-humectants-pull-water-from-deeper-layers-of-the-skin"/> ).
      <idx><h>triglyceride</h></idx> <idx><h>glycerol</h></idx> <idx><h>fatty acid</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      Unlike water, fats do not boil away at <quantity><mag>100</mag><unit base="celsius"/></quantity>.
      They can reach <quantity><mag>180</mag><unit base="celsius"/></quantity> to <quantity><mag>200</mag><unit base="celsius"/></quantity>, making frying and searing possible.
      This makes oils incredibly important for cooking.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-fatty-acids-differ-on-saturation">
    <title>Fatty Acids Differ on Saturation</title>

    <figure xml:id="fig-saturated-and-unsaturated-fatty-acid">
      <caption>The two major types of fatty acids.</caption>
      <image source="images/ch-03-the-kitchen/sec-water-and-lipids/saturated-and-unsaturated-fatty-acid.png" width="80%"/>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Fatty acids vary in their structure.
      If the hydrocarbon chain only contains single covalent bonds (i.e., the carbons only share one pair of electrons with each other), then the fatty acid is <term>saturated</term>.
      Otherwise, it contains double bonds and is <term>unsaturated</term>.
      Fatty acids with one double bond are <term>monounsaturated</term>, and fatty acids with more than one are <term>polyunsaturated</term>.
      <idx><h>fatty acid</h><h>saturated</h></idx> <idx><h>fatty acid</h><h>monounsaturated</h></idx> <idx><h>fatty acid</h><h>polyunsaturated</h></idx>
    </p>
    <aside>
      <title>Numbering carbons in fatty acids</title>

      <p>
        The carbon atoms are labeled <q>omega</q> or <m>\omega</m> starting from the carbon at the end.
        Chemists number fatty acids from the very end of the hydrocarbon tail, calling it the <term>omega carbon</term>.
        Counting inward from this tail end, we label the positions carbon 1, carbon 2, and so on.
        An <term>omega-3 fatty acid</term> simply has its first double bond located at the third carbon from that tail end.
        <idx><h>omega-3 fatty acid</h></idx>
      </p>
    </aside>

    <p>
      As <xref ref="fig-saturated-and-unsaturated-fatty-acid"/> shows, the hydrocarbon chains of saturated fatty acids are straight and linear.
      These molecules can therefore stack tightly together into dense structures even at room temperature.
      Butter, lard, and coconut oil are examples of saturated fats.
      <idx><h>butter</h></idx> <idx><h>lard</h></idx> <idx><h>coconut oil</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      In contrast, the double bonds in unsaturated fats introduce bends called kinks into the chain.
      These kinks prevent tight stacking, keeping them liquid oils at room temperature.
      <idx><h>kink</h></idx>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-butter-and-olive-oil">
      <caption>A comparison of what butter and olive oil are made of.</caption>
      <image source="images/ch-03-the-kitchen/sec-water-and-lipids/butter-and-olive-oil.png" width="80%"/>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-butter-vs-oil-melting">
      <title>The Room-Temperature Melt Test</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Take a tablespoon of olive oil and a tablespoon of butter straight from the refrigerator and leave them on the counter at room temperature ( <quantity><mag>21</mag><unit base="celsius"/></quantity> ) for thirty minutes.
          What happens to their physical states?
          Why?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The olive oil remains a clear, flowing liquid, while the butter softens but stays a moldable solid block.
        </p>

        <p>
          Butter consists primarily of saturated fatty acids whose straight hydrocarbon chains stack neatly and form strong intermolecular attractions, holding them together as a solid matrix at room temperature.
          Olive oil is rich in monounsaturated oleic acid; the double bond introduces a rigid kink that pushes neighboring molecules apart, preventing them from solidifying until chilled far below room temperature.
        </p>

        <p>
          <xref ref="fig-butter-and-olive-oil"/> compares the structure of the two. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
